A series of dinuclear paddlewheel-structured MOFs were explored.[47–49] Dybtsev et al.[47] synthesized a paddlewheelstructured $\mathrm{\Delta[Zn_{2}(B D C)_{2}(D A B C O)]\cdot4D M F\cdot0.5H_{2}O}$ $\scriptstyle\left(\mathrm{DABCO}=\right.$ 1,4-diazabicyclco[2.2.2]octane). The framework is composed of dinuclear $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{2}$ units with a paddlewheel structure, which are bridged by BDC dianions to form a distorted 2D square-grid $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}]$ . The axial sites of the $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}$ paddlewheels are occupied by DABCO acting as pillars to extend the 2D layers into a 3D structure (Fig. 5). Although BDC is generally considered to be a linear and rigid linker, the linker in this structure is bent, leading to severe twisting of the $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}$ paddlewheel from an ideal square grid. Interestingly, after evacuation of guest molecules, the BDC ligands linking the $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}$ paddlewheel units became linear, which resulted in a perfect 2D square grid of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}]$ . Such a paddlewheel-structured MOF, which has a high BET surface area of $1450\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , exhibited an adsorption capacity of $2.0\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. Furthermore, Chun et al. combined various aromatic dicarboxylates,[50] including BDC, tetramethylterephthalate (TMBDC), 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylate (1,4-NDC), tetrafluoroterephthalate (TFBDC), 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate (2,6-NDC), DABCO, and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dipyridyl (BPY), to form paddlewheel-structured frameworks $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ $\mathrm{\Delta[Zn_{2}(B D C)(T M B D C)(D A B C O)]}$ , $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TMBDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ , $[\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}_{2}$ $(1,4\mathrm{-NDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ ], $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TFBDC})_{2}(\mathrm{DABCO})]$ , and $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{TMBDC})_{2}$ (BPY)]. These frameworks possess surface areas in the range of $1450{-}2090\mathrm{m}^{2}\quad\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and hydrogen-adsorption capacities of $1.7{-}2.1\mathrm{wt}\%$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. In addition, using BDC, triethylenediamine (TED), and DMF, Lee et al.[48] synthesized the framework $[\mathrm{Zn(BDC)(TED)_{0.5}}]\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot0.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ . This framework, which also possesses a paddlewheel structure, adsorbed $2.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ of hydrogen at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. These results indicate that paddlewheel-structured dinuclear $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs have almost the same hydrogen capacity, which indicates that the type of organic linker does not have an obvious effect on the hydrogen uptake of a paddlewheel-structured $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF.
Lee et al. examined hydrogen adsorption on a trinuclear framework $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})_{3}\mathrm{BPY}]\cdot4\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathsf{B P D C}=$ biphenyldicarboxylate).[51] The structure of the framework possesses two crystallographically independent zinc centers (Zn1 and $z\mathrm{n}2$ ). Two $Z\mathrm{n}1$ atoms and one Zn2 atom form a trinuclear metal cluster $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})_{6}(\mathrm{BPY})_{2}]$ , in which one octahedral metal (Zn2) is located at the center and two tetrahedral metals (Zn1) are situated at two ends. The metal nodes are connected to adjacent nodes by carboxylate groups from six BPDC ligands located in the equatorial plane to form 2D double layers. The remaining two apical positions of the Zn1 are bound to the nitrogen atoms of BPY to form a 3D pillared framework. As a result, its BET surface area is $792\mathrm{m}^{2}{\bar{\mathrm{~\bf~g}}}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the hydrogen-adsorption measurements showed that it could adsorb $1.74\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. The high density of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ falls in the range of liquid $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which suggests relatively strong sorbent–sorbate interactions in the material.
## 2.2. Cu-Based MOFs
The synthesis of copper(II)-based MOFs also attracted much attention.[38,39,52–61] The first Cu–organic framework is HKUST-1 (also called CuBTC, where $\mathtt{B T C}=1,3,5$ -benzene tricarboxylate), which was invented by Williams et al.[52] and subsequently studied by numerous research groups.[38,39,53–57] HKUST-1, which is composed of $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ paddlewheel clusters linked by trigonal benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate, possesses a facecentered- cubic crystal that contains an intersecting 3D system of a bimodal pore size distribution with a BET surface area of about $1500\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The advantage of using copper(II) is that its property toward John–Teller distortion weakens the bonding of nucleophiles (such as solvent molecules) at the axial sites. The removal of these species can create the open metal sites and the $\mathrm{Cu^{8+}{-}O^{8-}}$ dipoles on the surface, which leads to an increase in the local interaction energy for hydrogen or other adsorptives. Indeed, the isosteric heat of hydrogen adsorption at low coverage is $1{-}2.0\mathrm{kJ}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ larger for HKUST-1 than for MOF-5.[38,39] As a result, the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed by HKUST-1 at 1 bar and 77 K was approximately double that of MOF-5.[39,46] However, at higher pressures, MOF-5 had a much higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed amount than HKUST-1. This occurred because the adsorbed hydrogen amount at low pressure strongly depends on the binding strength of $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}$ to the frameworks, whereas the amount adsorbed at higher pressure is mainly determined by the surface area.[62] Furthermore, the preparation and activation processes can have a significant impact on hydrogen adsorption capacity, surface area, and pore volume of HKUST-1.[63] The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes on HKUST-1, which were measured by different groups under nominally the same conditions, vary considerably for two possible reasons: crystal defects and the presence of guest molecules (contaminants) in the HKUST-1 samples.[63] For example, Liu et al. found the removal of all solvent from HKUST-1 by an improved activation process can increase its maximum hydrogen uptake up to $4.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 26 bar and 77 K.[63]
Yaghi et al. reported a new Cu–organic framework, $\mathrm{[Cu(L^{\bar{1}})(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ $\boldsymbol{{[\mathrm{{L}^{1}=}}}$ biphenyl- $\cdot3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic).[53] The framework (referred to as MOF-505) has a crystal structure in which ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ is coordinated by five O atoms in a square pyramidal geometry. Pairs of $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ centers are bridged by four carboxylate groups to form $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}]$ paddlewheel units. A $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule binds to each Cu center along the paddlewheel axis. Each $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{4}]$ paddlewheel is linked to four biphenyl liagnds and vice versa. After activation at $393\mathrm{K}$ , MOF-505 with a surface area of $1830\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ can reversibly adsorb $2.47\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar.[53] This adsorbed amount is comparable to that of HKUST-1. When the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure increased to 20 bar, the uptake of hydrogen on MOF-505 reached $4.02\mathrm{wt\%}$ .[54] To examine the effects of organic linkers, Lin et al. replaced the biphenyl- $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetracarboxylic $(\mathrm{L}^{1})$ of MOF-505 with the terphenyl- $3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic $(\mathrm{L}^{2})$ and the quaterphenyl- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime\prime\prime}}$ , $5{,}5^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic $(\mathrm{L}^{\dot{3}})$ , to form two new frameworks $\mathrm{[Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{L}^{2})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ (1) and $\mathrm{[Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{L}^{3})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ (2).[54] Frameworks 1 and 2 have higher surface areas of 2247 and $2932\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. The pore sizes are narrowly distributed around 6.5, 7.3, and $8.3\mathring\mathrm{A}$ for MOF-505, framework 1, and framework 2, respectively. At 1 bar (or below) and 77 K, MOF-505 had the highest hydrogen uptake, framework 2 the lowest, and framework 1 in between. However, when the pressure was above 2.5 bar, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of the later two exceeded that of MOF-505. Furthermore, at 20 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ , the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes of frameworks 1 and 2 reached 6.06 and $6.07\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. Fitting the high pressure region of their $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotherms to the Langmuir equation gave a maximum adsorption of 4.2, 6.7, and $7.01\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for MOF-505, framework 1, and framework 2, respectively.[54] This indicates that the hydrogen adsorption is dependent on the pore size (related to affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ) at a low pressure, whereas the surface area is a key factor in controlling the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption at a high pressure.
Zhou et al. reported the synthesis and structure of framework $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{TATB})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}$ $\langle\mathrm{TATB}={4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -s-triazine-2,4,6-triyltribenzoate).[58] Its structure possesses dicopper tetracarboxylate paddlewheel secondary building units (SBUs). With axial aqua ligands, which are linked by TATB bridges. This framework adsorbed about $1.9\mathrm{wt\%}$ of hydrogen at 1 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ .
