## 2.3. Mn-Based MOFs
Moon et al.[65] reported $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NDC})(\mathrm{DEF})]_{n}$ , which is a 3D porous MOF generating 1D channels. $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NDC})(\mathrm{DEF})]_{n}$ has no free space, because the DEF molecules that coordinate the $\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions occupy the channels. However, the DEF can be removed to obtain desolvated $[{\mathrm{Mn}}({\mathrm{NDC}})]_{n}$ that contains accessible coordination sites on $\mathrm{{Mn}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ sites, which results in a surface area of $191\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $0.57\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 1 bar.[65]
Long et al. synthesized a new Mn-based framework, $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{DMF})_{6}]_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{BTT})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{12}]_{2}\cdot42\mathrm{DMF}\cdot11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot~20\mathrm{C}$ $20\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ by using the tritopic bridging ligand 1,3,5-benzenetristetrazolate $\bigl(\mathrm{BTT}^{3-}\bigr)$ .[66] This porous MOF possesses a cubic topology.[66] Crystals of the compound with a high surface area up to $21\mathrm{\dot{0}0}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ showed a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $6.9\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 90 bar. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake can further increase with pressure, because $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption did not reach its saturation at 90 bar. Such a high hydrogen uptake was attributed to its high adsorption heat $(10.1\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at zero surface coverage), which was directly related to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding at coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ centers within the framework.[66]
## 2.4. Cr or Al-Based MOFs
Several $\operatorname{Cr}$ or Al-based MOFs have been evaluated for hydrogen storage (Table 1).[67–70] Fe´rey et al.[67] investigated the hydrogen adsorption properties on the metal–benzenedicarboxylate $\mathrm{M(OH)(O_{2}C{\cdot}C_{6}H_{4}{\cdot}C O_{2})}$ $[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}]$ denoted as MIL-53 [MIL: material from Institute Lavoisier]. The frameworks exhibited 1D channels with large free diameters of about $8.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and a large BET surface area of $1100\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The chromium compound showed a maximal hydrogen capacity of $3.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 16 bar, whereas the aluminum one exhibited the capacity of $3.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ . Furthermore, they evaluated the hydrogen adsorption on the giant-pore Cr-based MIL-100 and MIL-101 (Fig. 7).[71] MIL-100 and MIL-101 were built up from carboxylate moieties (BTC for MIL-100 and BDC for MIL-101) and trimeric chromium(III) octahedral clusters that had removable terminal water molecules and, therefore, provided potential unsaturated metal sites in the structure. The smaller of the two types of cages in their architectures was delimited by 12 pentagonal faces and the larger by 16 faces (12 pentagonal and 4 hexagonal). Without guest molecules, the accessible diameters of the cages were 25 and $29\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for MIL-100 and 29 and $34\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for MIL-101. The MIL-100, which was previously outgassed at 493 K, had a Langmuir surface area of $2700\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a maximum hydrogen uptake of $3.28\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 26.5 bar. In the MIL-101a that was obtained by outgassing MIL-101 with the same approach as for MIL-100, a significant amount of $\mathrm{\BDCH}_{2}$ molecules was still within the pores, which implied that most of metal sites were still poisoned by ${\mathrm{BDCH}}_{2}$ molecules. Its Langmuir surface was $4000\mathrm{{\bar{m^{2}}g^{-1}}}$ . At 77 K, the isotherm of hydrogen adsorption exhibited a maximum capacity at $4.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ with a saturation plateau above 40 bar. Furthermore, if MIL-101a was further subjected to additional treatment to evacuate most of the $\mathrm{BDCH}_{2}$ in the pores, the resultant sample (denoted as MIL-101b) had only half of the metal sites that were poisoned by coordinated ${\mathrm{BDCH}}_{2}$ molecules. Its Langmuir surface area was as large as $5500\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}^{2}\ \ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ . The maximum hydrogen adsorption capacity of MIL-101b reached $6.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 80 bar and 77 K. The high adsorption capacity of MIL-101b was attributed to its high adsorption heat (9.3 to $10.0\mathrm{kJ}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at low coverage), which was larger than that of MIL-100 (5.6 to $6.3\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ at low coverage). The nature of the interaction between hydrogen molecules and the framework that gave rise to such high values for MIL-101b is due to the presence of strong adsorption sites within the microporous supertetrahedra (ST), probably at each corner close to the trimers of chromium octahedra.[71]
## 2.5. Ni-Based MOFs
Lee and Suh reported a robust Ni-based metal–organic open framework, $\mathrm{[Ni(cyclam)(BPYDC)]\cdot5H_{2}O}$ , which is constructed of linear coordination polymer chains made of the nickel– macrocyclic complex $\mathrm{[Ni(cyclam)](ClO_{4})_{2}}$ (cycl $\mathrm{\um}=1,4,8,11$ - tetraazacyclotetradecane) and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate $({\mathrm{BPYDC}}^{{\dot{2}}-})$ .[72,73] This framework exhibits permanent microporosity with a Langmuir surface area of $81\bar{7}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and pore volume of $0.37\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ .[72] It adsorbed $1.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ of hydrogen at 77 K and 1 bar. Forster et al. synthesized a Na/Ni-based framework $\mathrm{NaNi_{3}(O H)(S I P)_{2}}$ ( $\mathrm{SIP}=5$ -sulfoisophthalate), consisting of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{34}$ clusters bridged by SIP to form a 3D network.[74a] The dehydration of this framework at temperatures between 573 and $623\mathrm{K}$ generated a porous material with a BET surface area above $700\mathrm{\overline{{m}}^{2}~g^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, the dehydrated framework possesses accessible, coordinatively unsaturated ${\mathrm{Ni}}^{{\mathrm{II}}}$ sites, which leads to a high adsorption heat of $9.4{-}10.4\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ for the hydrogen molecule. However, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity of this Ni-based framework at 1 bar and 77 K is only $0.94\mathrm{wt\%}$ because of its medium surface area.
Dietzel et al. synthesized a Ni-based coordination polymer, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{DHTP})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(\mathrm{DHTP}=2,5$ -dihydroxyterephthalic), which is a 3D honeycomb-like network with channels of ${\approx}11\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ diameter and a Langmuir surface area of $\scriptstyle1083{\mathrm{m}}^{2}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[74] $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on the network at 77 K exhibited a type I profile, with which the Langmuir equation yields a saturation value of $1.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ hydrogen capacity.
Zhao et al. evaluated three Ni-based MOFs (denoted as M, E, and C) for hydrogen adsorption and desorption. M and E have the composition $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{BPY})_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}$ with linear chains of BPY bridging metal centers, which are connected by T-shaped BPYcoordination at the metal into pairs. These pairs were aligned parallel to each other in M and perpendicular in E to form maximum pore cavity dimensions of $\mathbf{\widetilde{8.3\AA}}$ .[33] The cavities of MOFs M and E are connected by narrower windows, but the dynamics of the bridging BPY molecules confer sufficient flexibility on the framework to allow adsorptives (that appear oversized from a static view of the structure) to pass through the windows and access the pores. $\cdot^{[75,76]}\mathrm{C}$ , with formula $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(B T C)_{2}(3\mathrm{\cdotPIC)_{6}(P D)_{3}}}$ (where $\mathrm{PIC}=3$ -picoline and $\mathrm{PD}=$ propane-1,2-diol), has considerably larger windows and cavities of up to $14\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in size.[77] The adsorption–desorption isotherms for E and M showed a marked hysteresis, in which the former showed virtually no desorption even when the pressure was reduced from 1 to 0.01 bar.[33] In contrast, the adsorption–desorption isotherms of C did not show any substantial hysteresis up to 14 bar.[33] The maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptakes (at 77 K and 1 bar) on E, M, and C were 0.8, 0.7, and $2.1\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively.[33] Notably, the hysteresis in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in porous MOF materials, in which the pore window dimensions are similar to the kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , differs qualitatively from more rigid classical sorbents. Hydrogen can be loaded at high pressure and stored at low pressure, if the cavities are larger than the windows, which in turn are both close in size to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and have sufficient flexibility due to framework dynamics to allow kinetic trapping of the guest molecule.[33] Therefore, the design of MOF materials with thermally activated windows in the open channel structure provides a new possibility to improve their hydrogen-storage characteristics by modifying the desorption kinetics.
## 2.6. Other MOFs
Perles et al. synthesized a 3D polymeric terephthalate of scandium $\mathrm{[Sc_{2}(C_{8}H_{4}O_{4})_{3}]}$ under hydrothermal conditions by the reaction of ${\mathrm{Sc}}^{3+}$ with a mixture of terephthalic acid and disodium terephthalate. The structure consists of a polymeric 3D framework, in which each scandium atom is octahedrically coordinated to six carboxylic oxygen atoms of six different terephthalate anions.[78] Such a structured $\mathrm{[Sc_{2}(C_{8}H_{4}O_{4})_{3}]}$ has a BET surface area of $721\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a micropore volume of $0.293\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[78] It exhibited a hydrogen uptake of $1.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 0.8 bar.[78]
Kaye and Long examined hydrogen adsorption on dehydrated Prussian blue analogues of the type $\mathrm{M}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Fe,
Co, Ni, Cu, $Z\mathrm{n}$ ), wherein interactions with bridging cyanide ligands and/or coordinatively unsaturated metal centers lead to higher adsorption heat.[79] They reported that the BET surface areas range from $560\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ to $870\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ . The hydrogen uptake for the cyano-bridged frameworks varied from $1.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ in $Z\mathrm{n}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ to a maximum of $1.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ at 77 K and 1 bar. The heat of hydrogen adsorption is in a range of $5.9\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ to $7.4\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ .[79]
Lee et al. synthesized a trinuclear $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})_{3}\mathrm{BPY}]$ $4\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ framework, which has a similar structure to $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})_{3}\mathrm{BPY}]\cdot4\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ .[51] The hydrogen uptake of the $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})_{3}\mathrm{BPY}]$ 4DMF $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ framework is $1.98\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 1 bar, which is higher than the $1.74\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})_{3}\mathrm{BPY}]\cdot4\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The difference in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake between the two frameworks can be attributed to their different surface areas $(922\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the Co-based framework and $792{\mathrm m}^{2}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the Zn-based framework), pore volumes $(0.38\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the former and $0.33\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the later), and strengths of gas–solid interactions.[51]
Dinca˘ and Long reported the synthesis of $\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(\mathrm{NDC})_{3}(\mathrm{DEF})_{4}$ $(\mathrm{NDC}=2,6$ -naphthalenedicarboxylate), which is the first porous MOF incorporating $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ .[80] Its structure consists of linear $\mathrm{Mg}_{3}$ units linked by NDC bridges to form a 3D framework, featuring 1D channels filled with DEF molecules. Such a 3D framework structure is fully analogous to that of $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{NDC})_{3}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{2}$  $2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , which indicates that $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ can directly substitute for the heavier $Z\mathrm n^{2+}$ .[80] The desolvation of $\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(\mathrm{NDC})_{3}(\mathrm{DEF})_{4}$ by heating at $190^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ generated the microporous solid $\mathrm{Mg}_{3}(\mathrm{NDC})_{3}$ . This microporous framework has a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption heat $(7.0{-}9.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ). However, it exhibited a small hydrogen uptake of $0.48\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 1 bar because of its small BET surface area $(190\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ .[80]
## 3. Hydrogen Storage in MOFs at Ambient Temperature
As discussed in the above section, MOFs have a high hydrogen uptake (even higher than $7\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) at a temperature of $77\mathrm{K}.$ However, the hydrogen capacities of these MOFs are usually below $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ at ambient temperature with acceptable pressures (Table 2).[24,32,41,54,66,71,81–83] Such a low hydrogen uptake at ambient temperature is due to a weak binding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to MOFs. According to Bathia and Myers,[84] a thermodynamic requirement for an adsorbent capable of storing hydrogen at ambient temperature is $15.1\mathrm{kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ of hydrogen-adsorption heat. However, the heat of hydrogen adsorption reported to date for the MOFs are usually in the range of $4{\mathrm{-}}7{\mathrm{~kJ~}}{\mathrm{~mol}}^{-1}$ with the highest value of $7.0{-}12\mathrm{kJ}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at low surface coverage.[66,71] Several approaches have been employed or proposed to increase the interactions between hydrogen and MOFs.
## 3.1. Optimization of Cations
