binding energy with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .[24,32,41,54,66,71,83,85] Searching effective metals for MOFs is a high priority for synthetic chemists. So far, the Mn-based and $\mathrm{Cr}$ -based MOFs exhibit the high heats $(10\mathrm{kJ~mol~}^{-1})$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule adsorption.[66,71] Even these high heats are still much lower than $15.\bar{1\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}}$ , resulting in only about $0.43\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake (for Cr-based $\mathsf{M O F})^{[71]}$ and $1.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ uptake (for Mn-based $\mathrm{MOF})^{[66]}$ at ambient temperature and a pressure of about 80 bar. However, from the density functional theory (DFT)-Perdew–Burke–Erznerhof (PBE) calculations,[86] Sun et al. found that the binding energy to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can be tuned from about 10 t $)50\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ by using different transition metals in the MOF systems. The binding energies to hydrogen molecules were 10.4, 21.9, 34.6, and $\bar{46.5\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}}$ for Mn, Sc, Ti, and V in MOFs, respectively. However, because calculation results are strongly dependent on calculation approaches and selected models, it would be important to verify those theoretical predictions by experimental measurements.
## 3.2. Doping Li into MOFs
Recently, the effect of Li on properties of MOFs was explored by several theoretic research groups.[81,87–91] The following theoretical predictions for the effect of Li on hydrogen in MOFs at room temperature are very promising. However, no experimental data are yet available in the literature to support these predictions.
Han and Goddard’s grand canonical ensemble Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations demonstrated that doping of MOFs with Li is an effective way to increase the binding of $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}$ to MOFs.[81] Their GCMC simulations were based on the first-principles-derived force field (FF). This FF-based GCMC technique was validated by the fact that the results obtained from the FF-GCMC simulations were in a good agreement with experimental data for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on MOFs without Li. Han and Goddard employed this novel technique to simulate five Li-doped MOFs (Fig. 8). They found that Li atoms preferred to bind at centers of the hexagonal aromatic rings, but Li atoms on adjacent aromatic rings were on opposite sides. Furthermore, their results showed that at $300\mathrm{K}$ , Li-MOF-C30 (molar ratio of C to Li is 5) had $3.89\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at 20 bar and $4.56\mathrm{wt\%}$ uptake at 50 bar, which is the highest reversible hydrogen-storage capacity at room temperature yet reported. In contrast, the corresponding hydrogen uptakes of pure MOF-C30 were only 0.25 and $0.56\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 20 and 50 bar, respectively. This indicated that doping of MOFs with Li can increase hydrogen uptake at ambient temperature by almost 10 times. Furthermore, when the pressure of hydrogen went up to 100 bar, Li-MOF-C30 could store $5.16\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at 300 K, $5.57\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $273\mathrm{K}$ , and $5.99\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $243\mathrm{K}$ , reaching the DOE 2010 targets of $6\mathrm{wt\%}$ at a temperature between 243 and $353\mathrm{K}$ ( $-30$ and $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ). The presence of Li is the critical factor for high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at ambient temperature. For the MOFs without Li, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule bound weakly to both the metal oxide clusters and the aromatic linkers, resulting in small binding energies of 6.3 and $3.8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively. As a result, remarkable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in pure MOF systems can be obtained only at a temperature of $77\mathrm{K}$ or below. In contrast, for the Li-MOFs, the high electron affinity of the aromatic $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ carbon framework can create the positive Li sites, leading to a strong stabilization of molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Indeed, Li-doped MOFs exhibited high binding energies of 16.7 kJ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which results in a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake at ambient temperature. Such a strong interaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and Li-doped MOFs was supported by the DFT calculations and GCMC simulations from other groups.[87,88] However, Blomqvist et al. found that, although each Li on the organic linkers is able to bind up to three $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules, only two hydrogen molecules can successfully coordinate to each Li because of dynamical effects that prevent the third $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule from being adsorbed.[88] In other words, the dynamical effects may decrease the hydrogen capacity on Li-doped MOFs.
Although the above theoretical predictions have not yet been examined experimentally, the promoting effect of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on a twofold interwoven MOF was confirmed by Mulfort and Hupp.[92,93] They carried out experimental measurements for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})_{2}(\mathrm{diPyNI})$ 1 $(\mathrm{NDC}=2,0$ -naphthalenedicarboxylate; $\dim\operatorname{\mathrm{{qup}}}_{\operatorname{\mathrm{{yNI}}}}=$ $\mathrm{\DeltaN,N^{\prime}}$ -di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8- naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide) with and without ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ doping at $77\mathrm{K}$ .[92,93] Their results showed that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})_{2}(\mathrm{diPyNI})$ was $0.93\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 1 bar, but $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ -doped $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})_{2}$ (diPyNI) had nearly double the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacit $\zeta(1.63\mathrm{wt\%})$ ). A similar effect of Li on hydrogen storage in MIL-53(Al) was also observed.[94] The adsorption capacity of hydrogen was 1.7 and $0.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 77 K and 1 bar for MIL-53(Al) samples with and without Li, respectively.[94] This indicates that the hydrogen uptake of Li-doped MIL-53(Al) is larger than that of pure MIL-53(Al). However, these results were obtained at a very low temperature of 77 K and a low pressure of 1 bar. The effect of Li on hydrogen storage in MOFs is still waiting for experimental confirmation at ambient temperature and high pressure.
## 3.3. Doping MOFs with Catalysts
Breakthrough research has been done by Li and Yang.[95] They developed a novel approach that can markedly enhance hydrogen capacities by doping $\mathrm{Pt/AC}$ (activated-carbon) catalysts into MOFs. At 298 K and 100 bar, $1.6\mathrm{wt\%}$ reversible hydrogen capacity can be reached on the mechanical mixture of $\mathrm{Pt/AC}$ and MOF-5 and $1.8\mathrm{wt\%}$ on the mechanical mixture of $\mathrm{\Pt/AC}$ and IRMOF-8,[96] which were much higher than those without the $\mathrm{Pt/AC}$ catalyst (only about $0.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ ). The enhancement was attributed to atomic hydrogen spillover on carbon and secondary spillover on MOFs, that is, hydrogen molecules were dissociated into hydrogen atoms on Pt catalytic sites, followed by diffusion to the carbon surface and then to the MOF surface. Furthermore, the reversible hydrogen capacity can reach even $4\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 100 bar, if $\mathrm{Pt/AC}$ and IRMOF-8 were well-connected by more carbon bridges generated by sucrose carbonization (Fig. 9).[96] This is the highest experimental capacity of hydrogen yet reported for MOFs at ambient temperature. Furthermore, no apparent saturation value was approached for the sample as the isotherm was linear even at 100 bar. The absence of a saturation value suggests a further increase in capacity at higher pressures. The hydrogen uptake of $6\mathrm{wt\%}$ was predicted at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 150 bar. Such high hydrogen uptakes can be attributed to their high adsorption heats that ranged between 20 and $23\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ , which are higher than $15.1\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ .[96] This occurred because atomic hydrogen has a much stronger binding ability to both metal oxides and organic linkers than molecular hydrogen. They obtained similar results by using the combination of a $\mathrm{Pt/AC}$ catalyst with other MOFs (MOF-177, COF-1, HKUST-1, or MIL-101) via carbon bridges.[97,82] A comparison of these combinations indicated that the surface area and pore volume of the material were not the predominant factors that determined the hydrogen adsorption capacity by atomic hydrogen spillover.[97,82] However, a correlation between the hydrogen uptake by the spillover and the heat of adsorption of the material was found, which indicates the importance of the strength of the
